A context provider whose value reads the context it overrides accumulates: the
value is evaluated in the *enclosing* scope, so `depth(depth + 1)` reads the
value being overridden -- a strictly outer scope, which is what makes the
substitution terminate. Two nestings of it give 1 and 2, not 1 and 1.

The lowering installed the provider's value unlowered, so the read inside it
stayed an `*ir.ContextRead` and every backend printed it as the bare context
name: `string(depth + 1)` on both targets, naming an identifier the emitted
program never declares. That is what the denials below are about -- the wrong
numbers were the visible half and the undeclared identifier the serious one.

deny * `\bdepth\b` -- the context name is not a symbol any target declares; a read of it must have been substituted away
-- ctx.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"

output {
    none {
        html
    }
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
}

main()

context #depth(0)

component main() root {
    ui.window(title="t") {
        depth(depth + 1) {
            ui.text(value="one={depth}")
            depth(depth + 1) {
                ui.text(value="two={depth}")
            }
        }
    }
}
